(CSV TO EXCEL: KEEP BARCODE AS TEXT) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 716 Tm
(Import the CSV before Excel converts long barcodes into numbers.) Tj
/F2 13 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 680 Tm
(Why this matters) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 658 Tm
(CSV stores the barcode as digits, but opening a CSV directly can make Excel) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 642 Tm
(treat it as a number. Excel may then display a long barcode in scientific) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 626 Tm
(notation or change its digits. Import the Barcode column as Text instead.) Tj
/F2 13 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 588 Tm
(Import steps) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 566 Tm
(1. Open Excel to a blank workbook. Do not double-click the CSV file.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 542 Tm
(2. Select Data > From Text/CSV, then choose the downloaded CSV.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 518 Tm
(3. In the preview window, select Transform Data.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 494 Tm
(4. In Power Query, select the Barcode column.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 470 Tm
(5. Set its data type to Text \(not Whole Number or Decimal Number\).) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 454 Tm
(   If Excel asks, choose Replace Current.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 430 Tm
(6. Check Applied Steps. If a Changed Type step made Barcode numeric,) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 414 Tm
(   remove that step, then set Barcode to Text again.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 390 Tm
(7. Select Home > Close & Load to import the data into Excel.) Tj
/F2 13 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 346 Tm
(Check the result) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 324 Tm
(The Barcode cell should show the complete value, for example:) Tj
/F2 14 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 299 Tm
(7400000039262053840301) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 265 Tm
(If it shows something like 7.400000039E+21, close without saving and) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 249 Tm
(repeat the import, making sure the column is Text before Close & Load.) Tj
/F2 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 54 209 Tm
